# Normalize line endings in the repository, not just the working copy.
#
# The project is developed on Windows and built on Linux (Vercel, the GitHub Actions runner, and
# the Docker image). Without this, Git records whatever the contributor's platform produced, and a
# CRLF file committed from Windows can break a Linux build in ways that are very hard to trace
# back to a line ending - a shebang that the shell cannot read, or a Dockerfile continuation.
#
# `text=auto eol=lf` does both halves: check files into the repository with LF, and check them out
# on any platform with that platform's native convention. The repository stays canonical, which is
# what a build actually consumes.

* text=auto eol=lf

# Keep generated bundles out of the normalization rules entirely. They are not edited, and a
# sourcemap is a single enormous line that some tools choke on if it is rewritten.
*.map binary
server.mjs binary

# Shell scripts must never carry a carriage return: it becomes part of the shebang and the file
# stops being executable. `eol=lf` is enforced, not merely suggested, by adding `text`.
*.sh text eol=lf
Dockerfile text eol=lf

# Windows-only tooling. These are not read by the Linux build, so preserve them byte for byte.
*.ps1 text eol=crlf

# Binary assets must never be touched by the newline filter; doing so corrupts them.
*.png binary
*.jpg binary
*.jpeg binary
*.gif binary
*.webp binary
*.ico binary
*.woff binary
*.woff2 binary
*.ttf binary
*.mp3 binary
*.wav binary
*.pcm binary
